[DeepSeek]: <agent text removed for release> [Calling tool: complete_review]
2026-11-19 21:47:40 - Agent_20261119214626 - INFO - [*] Tool call: complete_review
2026-11-19 21:47:40 - Agent_20261119214626 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed the urllib3 1.26.17 redirect-handling query group covering CVE-2023-43804 (Cookie/Authorization stripping on cross-origin redirect) and CVE-2023-45803 (303 See Other request-body forwarding).

Findings submitted (4 total):

1. TRUE POSITIVE — src/urllib3/connectionpool.py:843-846 (CVE-2023-45803). In `HTTPConnectionPool.urlopen`, on a 303 the method is switched to "GET" but the request `body` (and accompanying `Content-Length`/`Content-Type` headers) is passed unchanged into the recursive `self.urlopen(method, redirect_location, body, headers, ...)` redirect at lines ~860-878. urllib3 1.26.17 lacks the `release_conn`/body-clearing logic added in 2.0.7/1.26.18 (`body = None; headers = None` for 303), so the original request payload is re-sent to the redirect target as a GET body. This leaks sensitive POST/PUT payloads (credentials, tokens, form data) to the redirect destination.

2. TRUE POSITIVE — src/urllib3/poolmanager.py:384-387. Same defect in `PoolManager.urlopen`: on HTTP 303 `method = "GET"` is set, but `kw` (which carries `body`) is forwarded unchanged to `self.urlopen(method, redirect_location, **kw)`, so the request body follows a cross-host redirect.

3. TRUE POSITIVE — src/urllib3/contrib/appengine.py:201. Same defect in `AppEngineManager.urlopen`: on 303 it sets `method = "GET"` and then re-invokes `self.urlopen(method, redirect_url, body, headers, ...)` without clearing `body`.

4. FALSE POSITIVE — src/urllib3/util/retry.py:237-238. `DEFAULT_REMOVE_HEADERS_ON_REDIRECT = frozenset(["Cookie", "Authorization"])` already includes Cookie AND Authorization, i.e. the CVE-2023-43804 fix is present in this tree (confirmed by test/test_retry.py:296 asserting {"authorization", "cookie"}), and `PoolManager.urlopen` correctly pops them for cross-host redirects. No header-stripping vulnerability here.

Not submitted as separate vulns: Query 2/3/4 results at connectionpool.py lines 646/685/798/824/849/858/876/879/888 are benign supporting artifacts/call-site markers for the same single 303-body defect already reported at line 843; they are not independent vulnerabilities.
