2026-11-19 21:50:26 - Agent_20261119214626 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed all 10 results of query 20261119214507 (py/retry-strip-impl) in src/urllib3/util/retry.py. The reported locations (lines 231, 238, 266-268, 271-272) are not vulnerable sinks but the Retry class's DEFAULT_ALLOWED_METHODS constant (HEAD/GET/PUT/DELETE/OPTIONS/TRACE), DEFAULT_REMOVE_HEADERS_ON_REDIRECT constant ("Cookie", "Authorization"), and deprecation-guard/deprecation-warning string literals for method_whitelist vs allowed_methods.

Verified the actual header-stripping implementation in src/urllib3/poolmanager.py:390-403 — PoolManager.urlopen joins the redirect location, then when retries.remove_headers_on_redirect is non-empty AND the redirect target is not the same host (conn.is_same_host), it pops all headers whose lowercased name is in remove_headers_on_redirect before recursing to the redirected URL. Values are normalized to lowercase in Retry.__init__ (lines 300-302) and propagated by Retry.new (line 317). So sensitive Cookie/Authorization (and any user-configured headers) are correctly stripped on cross-host redirects.

Additional confirmation: this tree is urllib3 1.26.17 (src/urllib3/_version.py), and CHANGES records 1.26.17 as "Added the Cookie header to the list of headers to strip from requests when redirecting to a different host" — i.e. the counterpart of GHSA/CVE-2023-43804 (Cookie header leak) is already fixed here. Cross-host redirects in connectionpool.urlopen raise HostChangedError rather than forwarding headers.

Therefore the query hits are information-only literals in non-vulnerable code; no true-positive credential-leak finding exists at these locations. No new vulnerabilities were discovered during the review.
